# AGENT 10 — HR & Admin Agent

This project builds only the **HR & Admin** agent (CV Screening, KPI, Payroll, Admin Control Panel). Other developers own sibling agents; all will later join into one multi-agent system.

## In scope (Critical — build these)

1. Job descriptions for all roles (Word / PDF)
2. CV scoring criteria per role (Word / Excel)
3. Attendance sheet format & rules (Excel template)
4. Payroll structure: base, overtime, deductions, advances (Excel / Word)
5. KPI definitions per department (Excel / Word)
6. Complete user role matrix — who accesses which agent (Excel)
7. Audit log requirements for admin panel (Word / SOP)

### Related system access (HR/Admin only)

- HR / Payroll software (if exists) — salary processing
- Attendance device / biometric system — pull attendance data

## Out of scope (Utilities & Maintenance agent — do not build here)

- Kitchen inventory & reorder levels
- IT equipment register
- Generator maintenance schedule & fuel log
- Solar system specs & monitoring
- Internet & utility account details
- IoT sensors on generator/solar
- IT asset management system

## Multi-agent integration (always remember)

Design this agent so it can later plug into a shared parent/orchestrator without a rewrite.

- Treat this agent as a **bounded capability module** with a clear public interface (inputs, outputs, events), not a closed monolith.
- Keep **identity, roles, and permissions** aligned with the shared **user role matrix** (item 6) — who can call this agent vs other agents.
- Prefer **stable contracts**: typed request/response shapes, shared event names, and documented handoff points to sibling agents.
- Leave **integration seams** (e.g. registry entry, message/event hooks, auth context from the parent) even if wired later as stubs/no-ops.
- Do **not** hardcode ownership of Utilities/Maintenance domains; route those concerns to the sibling agent when the orchestrator exists.
- When adding features, ask: "Will this still work when this agent is one of many under one admin control plane?"

## Working style

- Build in **stages/phases**; do not expand into out-of-scope domains.
- Prefer clear module boundaries over premature coupling to other agents' internals.

## Project structure (frontend / backend split)

- `backend/` — Python. FastAPI app (`app/api/`) wraps the core pipeline (`app/ingestion`, `app/parsing`, `app/scoring`, `app/ranking`, `app/reporting`, `app/pipeline.py`). SQLite data lives in `backend/data/`, config in `backend/config/`, OAuth/API secrets in `backend/credentials/` (gitignored). `app/integration/interface.py` is the only module a future orchestrator or sibling agent should import from.
- `frontend/` — React + Vite + TypeScript admin dashboard (`src/pages`, `src/components`, `src/api`). Talks to the backend only via the REST API (`VITE_API_BASE_URL`), never imports backend code directly.
- Keep this split for every future phase (attendance, payroll, KPIs, admin control panel): new backend capabilities get their own module under `backend/app/` + routes under `backend/app/api/routes/`, and corresponding pages/components under `frontend/src/`.
